<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiglath-pileser III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 745–727 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiglath-pileser III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 745 to 727 BCE and the architect of the Neo-Assyrian imperial system. He reorganised provincial administration into smaller units under royal governors, created a standing army, and greatly expanded the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportation</a>. Accounts of the Assyrian Empire treat his reforms as the foundation of Assyria's economy, military power and governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii.jpg" alt="Tiglath-pileser III" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilglath_pileser_iii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tiglath-Pileser III took his name, Tukultī-apil-Ešarra, meaning my trust belongs to the son of Ešarra, probably as a regnal name on accession. Ešarra was a temple of the god Ninurta, who was viewed as the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-god">Ashur</a>. Later sources call him Pul or Pulu. How he came to the throne is unclear and disputed. The Assyrian King List names his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nirari-v">Ashur-nirari V</a> as his father, while his own inscriptions call him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii">Adad-nirari III</a> and so Ashur-nirari's brother. There was a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> around 746 or 745, and many historians conclude he was a usurper. Karen Radner thinks that his claims of royal descent were probably true and that he won an intra-dynastic civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tiglath-Pileser curbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, the officials and generals whose influence had grown. He split the large provinces they governed into smaller ones under royally appointed governors, restricted the right to commission inscriptions to the king, and had the name of the official Shamshi-ilu erased from records. He turned the seasonal army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> into a professional one, with new weapons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>. The central standing army was called the kiṣir šarri, the king's unit, and was enlarged with troops from conquered lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p><p>His campaigns began in 744 against Babylonian lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. In 743 he created two new provinces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> and defeated the Urartian king Sarduri II in battle near Arpad, reversing the Assyrian defeat there eleven years earlier. He then conquered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, ending the kingdom of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and in 729 conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, becoming the first king to rule as sovereign of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Babylonia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> records his actions in the Levant. He used very brutal methods, including impaling and mass dislocation of populations, to keep control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He is believed to have died peacefully of old age, and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v">Shalmaneser V</a>, who was replaced after a few years by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>. By the time of his death he had more than doubled the lands under Assyrian rule. He made conquered lands Assyrian provinces rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdoms, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> came to depend on taxes collected by governors rather than on tribute. Several Assyriologists see his reign as the point at which Assyria truly became an empire, and later Middle Eastern empires, notably the Achaemenid, took the Assyrian empire as a model.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiglath-Pileser III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 740s BCE the reforms of <b>Tiglath-pileser III</b> broke up large provinces and turned many vassal states into directly administered territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian cuneiform inscriptions call the land Údumi or Údumu and name three of its kings: Kaus-malaka in the time of <b>Tiglath-pileser III</b> (c. 745 BCE), Aya-ramu in that of Sennacherib (c. 705 BCE) and Kaus-gabri in that of Esarhaddon (c. 680 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Against the standing armies of <b>Tiglath-pileser III</b> and his successors, however, Israel had no effective answer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Judah was repeatedly a vassal: Ahaz submitted to <b>Tiglath-pileser III</b> of Assyria around 734 BCE, and Manasseh, who ruled for about half a century, remained a loyal Assyrian tributary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its high point and its first great defeat came under Sarduri II, whose alliance with Arpad and other Syrian states was crushed by <b>Tiglath-pileser III</b> in 743 BCE; in 735 BCE the Assyrian king reached Tushpa but could not take the citadel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Military</a></li></ul></section>
